  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nLight winds and high RH values could bring some patchy fog this\nmorning, although mid to high level clouds look to limit this\nthreat. Some lower clouds may also be found in the southeast\nthis morning. Stalled surface front with northwest flow aloft \nwill bring a brief cool down today. High temperatures will \ngenerally be in the mid 70s to lower 80s, which will be slightly\nbelow normal for most locations. Wind will be less windy today\nwith a surface high to our north. The stalled front could bring\na few high based showers to the southwest today, with a low\nthreat for thunderstorms. Some haze or patchy wildfire smoke is\nalso possible today given the northwest flow aloft. Surface \nhigh moves across the region tonight bringing mainly clear skies\nand cooler lows in the 50s. Light winds combined with clear \nskies and cooler temperatures could bring some patchy fog, \nalthough confidence was not high enough to include at this time.\nWednesday through Thursday, surface high moves eastward while an\nupper level ridge begins to build back into the area. Southerly\nflow at the surface will begin to warm temperatures back to\nnormal to perhaps slightly above normal readings. Dewpoints\nshould remain low, limiting the heat risk. Depending on\nplacement of the upper level ridge, northwest flow aloft could\nbring some weak waves in the flow and perhaps some low\nprecipitation chances. Instability looks limited on Wednesday,\nalthough becomes slightly more increased on Thursday, with \nperhaps and isolated thunderstorm possible. Right now the NBM\nhas a mainly dry forecast, with better chances for \nprecipitation in South Dakota. \n\nFriday through the weekend, large upper level ridge looks to\nshift just enough to the east to return very hot temperatures\nand perhaps some dangerous heat risk. Forecast highs in the 90s\nto low 100s, and low temperatures in the 60s remain in the \nforecast. There could still remain some heating bias from the \nlast warming trend in the NBM, although the NBM spreads at least\nindicate another strong warming trend coming this weekend. Much\nlower dewpoints will be found for this upcoming weekend's heat.\nEven so Heat Risk and Wet Bulb Global Temperature values are \nshowing high to extreme heat risk. Heat Index Values may also be\nnear to above 100 degrees especially on Saturday. Will continue\nto monitor for potential heat products. Depending how strong \nthe ridge builds there could be near daily chances for showers \nand thunderstorms Friday through the weekend with any ridge \nrider type waves. These same disturbances could bring some \nincreased winds at times this weekend. This combined with low \nafternoon humidity values in the teens and 20s may also bring an\nincreased fire weather threat this weekend. Overall those with\noutdoor plans will want to start planning for a hot weekend with\nsome slight shower and thunderstorm chances.",
